# API Gateway Fix Walkthrough

I have implemented the fixes to resolve the `404 Not Found` errors and improve the health monitoring of your API Gateway.

## Changes Made

### 1. Added Root Route
**File:** [main.py](file:///Users/puneetkumar/Desktop/Kubernetes/E-Comm-New/services/api-gateway/main.py)
I added a `@app.get("/")` route to the FastAPI application. Now, requests to the root URL will return a friendly JSON response instead of a 404.

```python
@app.get("/")
async def root():
    return {
        "message": "Welcome to StreamShop API Gateway",
        "status": "online",
        "services": list(SERVICES.keys())
    }
```

### 2. Configured Health Probes
**File:** [deployments-fixed.yaml](file:///Users/puneetkumar/Desktop/Kubernetes/E-Comm-New/k8s/deployments-fixed.yaml)
I added `livenessProbe` and `readinessProbe` to the `stream-api-deploy` deployment. Kubernetes will now use the `/health` endpoint to check if the pod is alive and ready to serve traffic.

### 3. Synced Ingress Service Name
**File:** [ingress.yaml](file:///Users/puneetkumar/Desktop/Kubernetes/E-Comm-New/k8s/ingress.yaml)
I updated the backend service name from `api-gateway` to `stream-api-svc` to match the service definition in your deployment file. This ensures the Ingress controller can correctly route traffic to the gateway.

## Verification
- [x] API Gateway code updated.
- [x] Deployment YAML updated with probes.
- [x] Ingress YAML service name aligned.

> [!TIP]
> After applying these changes (using `kubectl apply -f k8s/`), wait a few moments for the pods to restart. You can check the logs again, and you should see `200 OK` for the health checks!
